CH = dict(num=1, title="Geographical Diversity of India", tag="Geography", theme="Himalayas, plains, desert, plateau, coasts, islands",
 intro="India is large, old and varied. Walking across its map from the snowy Himalayas to the coral islands shows how mountains, rivers, deserts, plateaus and coasts have shaped the way people live, farm, trade, build and worship. This chapter is a bird's-eye tour of those landforms.",
 big_questions=["What are the key geographical features of India?", "How does India's geographical diversity affect our lives?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "1.1 India on the Map"),
 ("ul", ["India is the **seventh-largest country** in the world and lies in **Asia**.",
  "With Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, Bhutan, Sri Lanka and Myanmar it forms the **Indian Subcontinent** (called a *sub*continent because it is a part of the continent of Asia).",
  "Natural boundaries: the **Himalayas** in the north, the **Thar Desert and Arabian Sea** in the west, the **Indian Ocean** in the south and the **Bay of Bengal** in the east. These separate India from the rest of Asia and have shaped its climate, culture and history.",
  "In 1984 **Rakesh Sharma**, India's first astronaut, told Indira Gandhi that India looks *'Sare jahan se achha'* from space."]),
 ("p", "For study, the book groups India into **five regions**: the great mountain zone, the plains of the Ganga and the Indus, the desert, the southern peninsula, and the islands. Always read the **legend (colour key)** of the physical map: each colour shows a height band."),
 ("h2", "1.2 The Himalayas"),
 ("ul", ["A wall-like range about **2,500 km** long, spread over **six countries**: India, Nepal, Bhutan, China, Pakistan and Afghanistan.",
  "Many peaks cross **8,000 m** and are called the **'Eight Thousanders'**; the highest mountain in the world, **Mount Everest**, is here.",
  "Called the **'Water Tower of Asia'**: summer snow-melt feeds the **Ganga, Indus and Brahmaputra** and their tributaries, which supply water for drinking, farming and industry to hundreds of millions.",
  "Mountains are held **sacred**; temples and monasteries attract pilgrims and seekers. The Bhagirathi (a main stream of the Ganga) begins at **Gaumukh**, the snout of the **Gangotri Glacier** in Uttarakhand.",
  "The name comes from Sanskrit **hima** (snow) + **alaya** (abode): *abode of snow*."]),
 ("h3", "How the Himalayas were formed"),
 ("ul", ["India was once part of the huge landmass **Gondwana**, next to Africa. It broke away and drifted **north**.",
  "About **50 million years ago** it collided with **Eurasia**; the land between crumpled and rose, like a carpet wrinkling when pushed. This is **folding**.",
  "India still pushes into Asia at about **5 cm a year**, so the Himalayas rise by roughly **5 mm a year** (about 5 m in a thousand years)."]),
 ("h3", "Three ranges of the Himalayas"),
 ("table", ["Range", "Also called", "Features", "Examples"], [
  ["Himadri", "Greater Himalayas", "Highest and most rugged; snow-covered all year; few settlements", "Mount Everest, Kanchenjunga"],
  ["Himachal", "Lower Himalayas", "South of Himadri; moderate climate, rich biodiversity, people live here", "Hill stations: Nainital, Mussoorie, Shimla, Darjeeling"],
  ["Shivalik", "Outer Himalayas", "Lowest and outermost; rolling hills, dense forests, wildlife; transition to the Gangetic plains", "Foothills along the plains"]]),
 ("ul", ["**Kath-kuni / dhajji-dewari** houses of the western Himalaya use local stone and timber: they keep warm and resist mild earthquakes.",
  "**Great Himalayan National Park** (Himachal Pradesh) is a **UNESCO World Heritage Site** with rich flora and fauna, protected by the government together with village communities. Wildlife includes the Himalayan monal, snow leopard and ibex.",
  "**Yaks** are reared for milk, meat, wool and dung and used for transport."]),
 ("h3", "Ladakh: the cold desert"),
 ("ul", ["Winter temperatures fall below **-30 °C**; rainfall is very low; terrain is rocky with deep valleys and lakes such as **Pangong Tso** (*tso* = lake; salty because of dissolved minerals).",
  "Called **'moonland'** for its moon-like landscape. The rocks were once under an ocean and are mostly sand and clay; **wind and rain eroded** them into strange shapes.",
  "Wildlife: snow leopard, ibex, Tibetan antelope. Culture: ancient monasteries and festivals such as **Losar and Hemis**."]),
 ("h2", "1.3 The Gangetic Plains"),
 ("ul", ["Flat, **fertile** land south of the Himalayas, watered by the **Ganga, Indus and Brahmaputra** systems. Rivers deposit **minerals (silt)** that enrich the soil.",
  "Ideal for **agriculture** (multi-cropping) and home to a **large share of India's population**; rivers also give hydroelectric power.",
  "Flat land made it easy to build **roads and railways**; rivers have been routes of **travel and trade** for thousands of years.",
  "Most rivers carry goddess names (Ganga, Yamuna, Kaveri); **Brahmaputra** means 'son of Brahma'. It swells in summer because of snow-melt in the Himalayas.",
  "Wildlife: Bengal tiger (revived by **Project Tiger**), gharial (endangered, protected by law), grey langur, peacock (**national bird**)."]),
 ("h2", "1.4 The Thar Desert and the Aravallis"),
 ("h3", "Thar (Great Indian) Desert"),
 ("ul", ["Spreads over **Rajasthan, Gujarat, Punjab and Haryana**; vast and arid, with **hot days and cold nights**.",
  "**Sand dunes** are shaped by wind and can rise to **150 m**. The desert is a natural barrier that discourages movement of people and animals.",
  "Adaptation: sand is used to scour utensils to save water; rinse water is reused; Rajasthan has **taanka / kunds** (rainwater-storage structures).",
  "**Jaisalmer**, the 'Golden City', has a fort that is a **UNESCO World Heritage Site**; the **Pushkar Mela** (edge of the Thar) is famous for camels."]),
 ("h3", "Aravalli Hills"),
 ("ul", ["Among the **oldest mountains in the world** (about **2.5 billion years**). Highest peak **Mount Abu** (over 1,700 m); most hills are 300-900 m.",
  "Spread through **Delhi, Haryana, Rajasthan and Gujarat**; they **stop the Thar from spreading east**.",
  "Rich in **marble, granite, zinc and copper**. The ancient mines at **Zawar** show that Indians were first to master **zinc extraction** over eight centuries ago.",
  "Forts: **Chittorgarh, Kumbhalgarh, Ranthambore**; hills gave natural defence."]),
 ("h2", "1.5 The Peninsular Plateau"),
 ("ul", ["A **plateau** is a raised land with a more or less flat top and steep sides. The triangular plateau of central-south India is surrounded by water on three sides (**peninsula**: Arabian Sea, Bay of Bengal, Indian Ocean), so it is the **Peninsular Plateau**, a very old landform.",
  "Bordered by the **Western Ghats** (taller, wall-like along the west coast, waterfalls in monsoon; northern part called **Sahyadri**; a **UNESCO World Heritage Site**) and the **Eastern Ghats** (lower, broken hills on the east).",
  "Between them lies the **Deccan Plateau**. The plateau tilts slightly **east**."]),
 ("table", ["Direction of flow", "Rivers", "Drain into"], [
  ["East-flowing", "Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri, Mahanadi", "Bay of Bengal"],
  ["West-flowing", "Narmada, Tapti", "Arabian Sea"]]),
 ("ul", ["Rich in **minerals (coal), forests and fertile soil**; waterfalls give **hydroelectric power** and irrigation. Coal is a fossil fuel and adds to global warming.",
  "Dense forests shelter tribal communities such as the **Santhal, Gond, Baiga, Bhil and Korku**, with their own languages and nature-linked traditions. Tribal communities are spread across Jharkhand, West Bengal, Odisha, Assam, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Maharashtra, Telangana and Gujarat.",
  "Western Ghats biodiversity: lion-tailed macaque, king cobra, insect-eating plants. **Mumbai** on the west coast is India's financial centre."]),
 ("h2", "1.6 India's Coastlines"),
 ("p", "India's coastline is **over 7,500 km** long, with beaches, cliffs, forests and coral reefs."),
 ("table", ["Feature", "West Coast", "East Coast"], [
  ["Stretch", "Gujarat to Kerala through Maharashtra, Goa, Karnataka", "Ganga delta to Kanyakumari"],
  ["Lies between", "Western Ghats and Arabian Sea", "Eastern Ghats and Bay of Bengal"],
  ["Rivers", "Short, swift rivers from the Western Ghats; form **estuaries** (Narmada and Tapti are the largest)", "Rivers form fertile **deltas** (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri)"],
  ["Coast shape", "Narrow; coves, creeks, estuaries; hills close to the sea", "Wide plains"],
  ["Water bodies / use", "Many ports and cities, centres of trade for thousands of years", "**Chilika** and **Pulicat** lakes (Pulicat is a lagoon)"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Delta**: a triangular or fan-shaped landform formed at a river mouth when sediments settle in a larger water body. **Lagoon**: water separated from the sea by a natural barrier. **Estuary**: the wide river mouth where river meets the sea without forming a delta."),
 ("h2", "1.7 The Indian Islands"),
 ("table", ["Island group", "Sea", "Nature", "Key facts"], [
  ["Lakshadweep", "Arabian Sea, near the Malabar coast of Kerala", "**Coral** islands; an archipelago of **36** islands", "Not all inhabited; gives India a large marine area for fishing and resources"],
  ["Andaman and Nicobar", "Bay of Bengal", "**500+ volcanic** islands in two groups", "Strategic outposts watching the ocean; **Cellular Jail** held freedom fighters; **Barren Island** is India's only active volcano"]]),
 ("h2", "1.8 The Sundarbans and the North-East Hills"),
 ("ul", ["**Sundarbans**: delta of the Ganga and Brahmaputra where river, sea and land meet; about half is in India, half in Bangladesh; **mangroves**; home of the **Royal Bengal Tiger**; a **UNESCO site**.",
  "**North-East hills (Meghalaya Plateau: Garo, Khasi, Jaintia)**: lush, one of the world's **highest-rainfall** regions, waterfalls (e.g., Seven Sisters).",
  "**Mawlynnong** (East Khasi Hills) is the 'cleanest village in Asia' with bamboo dustbins; **living root bridges** (near Nongriat, Cherrapunji) are made by weaving tree roots over many years; the Khasi festival **Shad Suk Mynsiem** thanks nature."]),
 ("h2", "1.9 Why Diversity Matters"),
 ("ul", ["Diverse landforms create different soils, plants, animals, jobs and cultures; food habits and food preservation adapt to local conditions (e.g., drying vegetables for the off-season).",
  "India is called a **'mini-continent'** because it holds mountains, plains, desert, plateau, coasts and islands in one country.",
  "Geography has shaped civilisation; rivers and shared land help link people into one nation."]),
 ("h2", "1.10 Quick Revision"),
 ("qa", [("Why are the Himalayas called the 'Water Tower of Asia'?", "Their snow-melt feeds the Ganga, Indus and Brahmaputra, which supply water to hundreds of millions of people."),
  ("How were the Himalayas formed?", "The Indian landmass broke from Gondwana, moved north and collided with Eurasia about 50 million years ago; the land between folded upward."),
  ("Name the three ranges of the Himalayas.", "Himadri (Greater), Himachal (Lower) and Shivalik (Outer)."),
  ("Why is Ladakh called a cold desert and 'moonland'?", "It has very low rainfall and winter temperatures below -30 °C, and its eroded rocky terrain looks like the moon's surface."),
  ("Why are the Gangetic plains densely populated?", "Rivers deposit fertile silt and give water; flat land supports farming, roads, railways and trade."),
  ("How do people of the Thar adapt to water scarcity?", "They use sand to clean utensils, reuse rinse water and store rain in taankas or kunds."),
  ("What role do the Aravallis play?", "They check the eastward spread of the Thar, hold minerals like marble, zinc and copper, and shelter forts such as Kumbhalgarh."),
  ("Why is the southern plateau called the Peninsular Plateau?", "It is surrounded by water on three sides: the Arabian Sea, Bay of Bengal and Indian Ocean."),
  ("Differentiate between east-flowing and west-flowing peninsular rivers.", "East-flowing rivers (Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri, Mahanadi) drain into the Bay of Bengal; west-flowing rivers (Narmada, Tapti) drain into the Arabian Sea."),
  ("Compare the west and east coasts.", "The west coast is narrow with estuaries and ports; the east coast is wider with large deltas and lakes like Chilika and Pulicat."),
  ("Name the two island groups of India with one feature each.", "Lakshadweep: coral islands in the Arabian Sea; Andaman and Nicobar: volcanic islands in the Bay of Bengal with Barren Island, India's only active volcano.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["India is the **7th-largest country** and part of the **Indian Subcontinent**.",
  "Five zones: **mountains, plains, desert, peninsular plateau, islands** (plus coasts).",
  "Himalayas formed by the **India-Eurasia collision ~50 million years ago**; three ranges: **Himadri, Himachal, Shivalik**.",
  "The **Gangetic plains** are fertile and populous; the **Thar** is a hot desert; the **Aravallis** are among the oldest hills.",
  "The **Peninsular Plateau** is flanked by the **Western and Eastern Ghats**; Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri flow east; Narmada and Tapti flow west.",
  "Coastline **7,500+ km**; west coast has estuaries, east coast has deltas.",
  "Islands: **Lakshadweep (coral, 36)** and **Andaman and Nicobar (volcanic, 500+)**; Barren Island is the only active volcano.",
  "Diversity gives India its mix of soils, livelihoods and cultures: a **'mini-continent'**."])
